{"name":"Frisky Whiskers","hex":"#bdb8bb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"frisky","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Abounding in energy or playfulness.","Sexually aroused."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/frisky"},{"term":"whiskers","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of whisker","Faded creases around the crotch of blue jeans. Can be added artificially or created by daily wear over the lifetime of a garment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/whiskers"}],"entries":[{"title":"Whiskers","blocks":[{"text":"Whiskers, also known as vibrissae, are specialized sensory hairs that help most species of mammals sense their environment. Whiskers function as highly sensitive feelers that help mammals navigate the world in many different ways, including the detection of air currents and physical objects to aid in navigation, hunting, and spatial awareness. Whiskers can trigger reflexes to protect the eyes. Whiskers also play a significant role in the communication between individual animals in different species of mammals. They convey an animal's emotional state, essentially sending a message to other members of their species about what emotion they are feeling at the time. Whisker movement can be thought of as a form of language that animals use to silently communicate with the other animals around them."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Cat_whiskers_closeup.jpg/330px-Cat_whiskers_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whiskers","wikidata":"Q913572"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Chinchilla-Patchouli.jpg/500px-Chinchilla-Patchouli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Chinchilla-Patchouli.jpg/500px-Chinchilla-Patchouli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A chinchilla with large macrovibrissae","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChinchilla-Patchouli.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Pseudalopex_culpaeus.jpg/500px-Pseudalopex_culpaeus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Pseudalopex_culpaeus.jpg/1280px-Pseudalopex_culpaeus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Pseudalopex_culpaeus.jpg/500px-Pseudalopex_culpaeus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Patagonian fox showing four major cranial groups of vibrissae: supraorbital (above the eye), mystacial (where a moustache would be), genal (on the cheek, far left), and mandibular (pointing down, under the snout)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APseudalopex_culpaeus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Rat_whiskers_2.jpg/500px-Rat_whiskers_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Rat_whiskers_2.jpg/1280px-Rat_whiskers_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Rat_whiskers_2.jpg/500px-Rat_whiskers_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A pet rat clearly showing the grid-like arrangement of the macrovibrissae on the face, and the microvibrissae under the nostrils. The supraorbital vibrissae above the right eye are also visible.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARat_whiskers_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Hpim0279.jpg/500px-Hpim0279.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&ut